# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 5ff2ef5 is EPIC L's browser evidence: backupd-tests' feat/816-workflow-
# e2e, carrying suites/web-ui's four workflow-*.spec.ts files and the
# Suite C equivalence rows for every mutating workflow route. It is a
# BRANCH HEAD rather than a merged main commit, which every paragraph
# above would normally refuse, and the reason is lockstep: the rig those
# specs need did not exist until this commit of THIS repository, so their
# branch cannot be merged against a main that cannot run it and this one
# cannot be proved against a pin that predates it. #816 lands both halves
# together, and their merge is the follow-up.
#
# What this pin buys, run through scripts/e2e/three-machine-web-ui.sh
# against a real stack on the machine this was written on: a Host
# Workflow Runner in its own container (the product's own binary, the
# host's Docker socket, non-root, socket and credential in volumes the
# engine mounts the way container/compose.yaml does), an exec host whose
# `hookuser` account genuinely runs a NAME.remote.sh, the VPS's
# internal-sftp-forced account genuinely refusing one, and twelve
# scenario backup sets whose hooks produce the states #816 is about --
# including the two the epic exists for: `backup=skipped workflow=failed`
# for a failing before hook, and `backup=success workflow=failed
# cleanup=failed` for a failing after hook.
#
# And what it does NOT buy, stated here because a reader of this file is
# the person who will next be surprised by it:
#
#   27 cases in suites/web-ui are RED, and they are red at
#   d04314490 too. That was measured rather than assumed: the same rig,
#   with --no-workflows so none of #816's fixture is in play, was run
#   against both this sha and the previous one, and the two failure lists
#   are identical -- sixteen wizard cases, four backup-sets, three
#   quarantine-activity, and one each in auth, dashboard,
#   provider-treatment and safety-invariants. They are product/suite
#   drift on their main, tracked in #913, and no bump on this side closes
#   them. This bump does not make the gate redder; it does not make it
#   green either.
#
#   And the run cases in workflow-run.spec.ts and workflow-terminal.spec.ts
#   cannot pass on ANY deployment this repository can build today, for a
#   reason that is not the suite's fault and not the rig's: POST
#   /api/v1/operations run_backup_set is refused 403
#   DESTRUCTIVE_OPERATIONS_DISABLED, because apps/common/webhost's only
#   DestructiveGate implementation is NotYetImplementedGate and its own
#   doc says there is deliberately no flag that can make it report true
#   until #92 is done. So a browser cannot start a backup on a real
#   deployment at all, and the specs that press "Run this backup set"
#   fail waiting for a run the engine was never going to be allowed to
#   start. The runs those cases want DO happen -- the scheduler makes
#   them on its own timer, which is what the evidence above was read from
#   -- so the fix is for those cases to observe a scheduled run rather
#   than to ask for one, and that is a change on their side. It is
#   written up in #816 rather than worked around here, because a rig that
#   opened that gate would be a rig proving something no operator can do.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# b9d3c40 is the sha #816 was proved against, and it is the first pin in
# this file with NO workflow case blocked behind an issue number.
#
# What moved between 5ff2ef5 and here was not the suite drifting: it was
# four defects the rig found in a browser, each of which had to be fixed
# in the product before the case that found it could assert rather than
# skip.
#
#   #916  the step log terminal measured 1178px in a 940px window and
#         the page scrolled sideways, with wrapping ON. Two halves: the
#         terminal was not capped at its column, and the run page's step
#         rows were a `repeat(auto-fit, minmax(130px, 1fr))` grid whose
#         min-content width was 130px times the facts a step carries --
#         about 1170px -- which the shell's `overflow: auto` main took
#         rather than clipped.
#   #917  coming back to a step re-read its log from the engine even
#         though the viewer still held the whole page, so the bounded
#         cache was an optimisation of memory alone.
#   #919  no remote hook had run since L2/L3. The engine passed the
#         step id as remoteexec's token, step ids carry a tilde, the
#         token rule refuses one before a session opens, and the
#         refusal was reported as transport_lost -- while the
#         token-less capability preflight said the connection was fine.
#         Fixed in #920.
#   and the validation spool this PR fixes, which reported every set
#   with a NAME.local.sh hook as having invalid hooks.
#
# Every one of those needed a real runner, a real exec-capable sshd and
# a real browser at once. That is the whole argument for this file
# existing, and it is the first time the argument has been paid back
# four times in one bump.
#
# Still red, and still not this pin's business: the 28 product/suite
# drift cases tracked in #913, which are red at every pin this file has
# ever named, including the one before this.
#
# 859be963 is the sha #816 merged on, and the last thing to move in it
# was the recovery pair becoming one case: "a crashed run is held, and
# both ways out of the hold work". Two cases each crashing the engine
# meant two waits for the slowest set in the deployment, and the second
# one starved -- a set with an unsettled hold is a set the scheduler
# will not run again, so the case that needed a live hook to crash into
# never got one. One crash, both settlements asserted against the one
# hold, is not a weaker claim: it is the same two exits from the same
# hold, proved against a run that was genuinely interrupted rather than
# against two that had to be manufactured in sequence.
#
# The other thing this pin records is worth more than the diff. Its
# remote-hook case requires the host the SCRIPT printed to appear in the
# executor the PRODUCT names, and when it went red it quoted both:
#
#   the hook printed that it ran on "exechost" and the product says it
#   executed on "Remote · exec-host", so one of them is describing a
#   different machine
#
# That was a fixture defect wearing a product defect's clothes -- two
# names for one machine, in this repository's rig, at a moment when the
# obvious reading was that remote execution was still broken. An
# assertion that had only checked for "some hostname" would have passed
# and proved nothing; one that compared against a literal would have
# failed without saying why. The evidence was in the failure text, and
# that is the argument for assertions that print what they saw.
#
# c30523ca is what #816 merged on, and the last thing to move was the
# interaction nobody had thought about: taking the Host Workflow Runner
# away is not scoped to a backup set.
#
# One case stops the runner to prove the validation report names it as
# the reason. It names e2e/workflow-happy, so it reads like a
# set-scoped act, and it is not: the runner is the facility every
# NAME.local.sh hook executes through, so the outage interrupts
# whichever set the scheduler happened to be mid-hook on. At this rig's
# cadence -- measured at a run per set every ~77s, twelve cycles in
# eight minutes -- that is very often the crash-fixture set, whose
# before hook deliberately holds for forty-five seconds. The
# interrupted run then owes a cleanup nobody settled, which is a HOLD,
# and a held set is correctly never scheduled again. Twenty-five
# minutes later the case that needs a live hook to crash into waited
# five scheduler intervals for a run that could not come, and reported
# it as the scheduler not running the set -- which was true, and whose
# cause was four files away.
#
# Both halves are fixed at the source rather than waited out: the
# outage settles what it caused across EVERY set and prints which ones
# it cleared, and the crash case clears any hold on its own set before
# it starts. Acknowledge, not resume, in both: a resume EXECUTES the
# owed hooks, and nothing in a cleanup path should be deciding what a
# hook does to a source machine.
#
# No assertion was relaxed and no budget grew, which is the only reason
# this pin is worth having: what changed is the arrangement, not the
# claim.
#
# 0b691c59 is the last move, and it is a WHEN rather than a what. The
# run before this one proved the shape from both ends: the deployment
# was asked for its facts on connect and answered "0 recovery hold(s)",
# and the same suite's reporter ended the run with "still holding 2
# workflow run(s) for recovery: e2e/workflow-crash,
# e2e/workflow-crash" -- two rows being one interrupted run held at
# both scopes, global and set, which is exactly what this rig produces
# by hand. So the hold was real, and every attempt to settle it had
# looked for it BEFORE it existed: an interrupted run does not raise
# its hold until it finalises, which is after the case that caused the
# outage has moved on and after a clear that ran once at another case's
# entry.
#
# Both ends now settle at a moment the hold can actually be found: the
# crash case acknowledges any hold on its own set on a callback while
# it waits, so a hold appearing mid-wait is a legitimate starting state
# rather than a race it lost, and the runner-outage case settles
# deployment-wide at its file's end, by which time the run it
# interrupted has finalised. Nothing waits longer and no assertion
# moved.
#
# The reason this is worth a paragraph rather than a line: every red in
# this sequence was diagnosed from a message a suite printed about what
# it saw -- the executor names that disagreed, the hold count at
# connect, the hold list at exit. None of it came from a debugger, and
# none of it would have existed if the assertions had been written to
# be easy to satisfy.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# e5eeb5ab is the rename, and it is the first bump in this file whose two
# halves could not have landed apart at all. EPIC R (#885) renames this
# product from `backupd` to `retnd`, and those suites assert on the CLI's
# own name, its usage block, its diagnostics and the paths it prints --
# so their expectations move with the binary. Measured over there before
# the merge: the PRE-sweep suite is 44 of 97 RED against a stamped build
# of the renamed tree, and the SWEPT suite is 59 of 97 RED against a
# build of the pin this line held two bumps ago. Either half alone is a
# red gate, which is what this file has been saying about every bump
# since a794c38 and is finally true in both directions at once.
#
# The `RM_*` environment contract went with it. Those 23 token-and-path
# pairs were another repository's interface and had been on
# check-brand-drift.sh's `preexisting` list since #794 for exactly that
# reason: renaming them here alone breaks the gate, and renaming them
# there alone breaks it too. They are `RETND_*` on both sides as of this
# bump, a straight prefix swap with NO read-compat alias, because the
# only writers are scripts/bdtools/e2e/run_tests_repo_gate.py and
# scripts/e2e/three-machine-web-ui.sh and the only reader is that
# harness -- a compat window would protect nobody and leave an undated
# shim, which is what FR-43 refuses. The `preexisting` entries are
# deleted rather than moved to `aliases`.
#
# ONE THING THIS BUMP DID THAT A NORMAL ONE DOES NOT, and it is the part
# worth reading if this file's history ever has to be reconstructed. The
# sha this line held before, 0b691c5, was a BRANCH HEAD of their
# feat/816-workflow-e2e and was never on their main -- the "follow-up
# merge" the paragraph above about 5ff2ef5 flagged as owed and nobody
# came back to. So the rename could not simply be branched off their
# main: a rename branched off main alone would have produced a pin with
# the rename and WITHOUT EPIC L's 3,596 lines of browser evidence, and
# the loss would have looked like a clean forward bump. That merge landed
# first, as backupd-tests#84, and deliberately as a MERGE rather than a
# squash so 0b691c5 stays a reachable ancestor and this file's own
# history still resolves. ced2cb3 therefore carries both.
#
# Verified at e5eeb5ab on a clean checkout: scripts/gate-local.sh green
# end to end -- source mode built ./cmd/retnd and ./cmd/retnd-web from
# 72c940e9 and the identity handshake agreed (rclone v1.75.1, go
# go1.27.1), Suite A ran 97 of 97 with no failures, both `tsc --noEmit`
# checks clean, Suite B 65 of 65 unit tests. Which it was not before:
# tools/getbuild's TestParseVersionIgnoresAnUnrelatedFirstWord was red on
# their main for a reason that had nothing to do with any pin, and was
# fixed in the same PR.
#
# The sha is e5eeb5ab rather than ced2cb3, the rename's own merge, and the
# difference between the two is README.md alone -- 25 insertions, 6
# deletions, `git diff --name-status` returning exactly `M README.md`, so
# it cannot move the gate either way. It is the one to pin because that
# README is where the tests repository enumerates FR-43's allowlist, and
# docs/conformance/epic-r-matrix.md cites it: a pin naming the tree whose
# README listed five of the eight surviving-old-name categories would
# have the matrix cite an allowlist the pinned sha does not contain,
# which is the same defect as a matrix row citing a suite that does not
# exist.
#
# The one-sided-bump control, watched rather than described, because that
# is this file's whole reason for existing: restoring the pin to 87a9dc7
# while the tree is renamed makes their `make build` fail with
# `core/cmd/retnd: directory not found`, since 87a9dc7's build-under-test
# names a tree whose command directory still carried this product's
# SECOND name, two renames back. A
# stale pin therefore yields NO run rather than a wrong one, which is the
# better of the two failure modes and not one anybody designed.
#
# Their build-under-test.json names 72c940e9 of this repository, which is
# this tree's main as the rename was proved against and predates the
# commit this bump lands in. That is the manual half every paragraph
# above flags, and this time it is deliberate rather than owed: what
# changes on this side after 72c940e9 is provenance records, the
# brand-drift guard's lists and the conformance matrix, none of which any
# of those suites observes. Nothing over there is red for it.
#
# Moved to c75e4dd, which is retnd-tests#87, and it is the other half of
# FR-41's cutover. That repository's `go.mod` still declared its module
# under the retired organisation while it was being fetched from
# `github.com/retnd/retnd-tests`, which is the same mismatch R1.3 (#888)
# closed on this side and which #895's own matrix row R2.18 claims of
# BOTH trees; #87 sweeps the module path, its thirteen import paths,
# getbuild's live clone URL, the `ghcr.io` path in four files and every
# absolute repository link, and leaves exactly one category standing --
# the issue and pull-request links that record where work was TRACKED,
# which a transfer preserves by number and redirects by URL, so rewriting
# them would falsify that record.
#
# It cannot move the gate either way and that is checkable rather than
# asserted: `git diff --stat` over #87 is 17 files, 28 insertions, 28
# deletions, all of them a coordinate string, and `go build ./...` and
# `go vet ./...` pass over there. No suite expectation, fixture, case
# YAML or harness assertion changed, and their own allowlist grep still
# returns 55 lines across 15 files.
#
# Their build-under-test.json still names 72c940e9 of this repository, for
# the reason the paragraph above already gives and one more: what has
# changed here since is provenance records, the brand-drift guard's lists,
# the conformance matrix and the coordinate sweep, and no suite over there
# observes any of them. The manual half is deliberate again rather than
# owed.
